.TH UA1000CTL 1 "November 2026" "ua1000ctl 0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ua1000ctl \- control the mixer, patch bay and panel switches of a UA-1000
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ua1000ctl
.RI [ option ]...
.I command
.RI [ argument ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ua1000ctl
changes one setting of a Roland/EDIROL UA-1000 audio interface: it updates
the editor's state, sends the messages that make the device follow, and saves
the state.
The state is shared with
.BR ua1000gui (1).
.PP
The device cannot report its mixer settings, so the state holds what the
editor has set.
.B status
prints it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIport\fR"
The ALSA rawmidi port, such as
.BR hw:2,0,1 .
By default the port named
.B "UA-1000 Control"
is used.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-state " \fIfile\fR"
The state file (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print each message in hexadecimal, one per line, instead of sending it; the
state file is not changed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the commands.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH COMMANDS
Channels are numbered 1 to 10; pairs are written
.BR 1\-2 ,
.BR 3\-4 ,
.BR 5\-6 ,
.BR 7\-8
and
.BR 9\-10 .
.B input
is the INPUT MONITOR mixer and
.B output
the OUTPUT MONITOR mixer.
.TP
.BI status
Print the state the editor knows, in the format of the state file.
.TP
.B read
Ask the device for its state and print it, in the format of the state file:
the panel switches, the patch bay, and both mixers and MONITOR OUT (gains,
pans, mutes, links).
The solos are not read (how the device reports them is not known) and are
printed off.
Nothing is sent but the request, and the state file is not changed.
.TP
.B ports
List the MIDI ports, marking the UA-1000.
.TP
.B sync
Send the whole state to the device.
.TP
.BR input | output " \fIchannel\fR " gain " \fIdB\fR"
Set a channel's gain:
.B \-inf
(either case)
or a level in dB up to +12, written in plain decimal (\-6, +1.5, 0.25), with an
optional
.B dB
suffix.
.TP
.BR input | output " \fIchannel\fR " pan " \fIposition\fR"
.BR L20 " .. " L1 ,
.BR C ,
.BR R1 " .. " R20 ,
in either case.
.TP
.BR input | output " \fIchannel\fR " mute | solo " " on | off
.TP
.BR input | output " \fIpair\fR " link " " on | off
While a pair is linked, gain, mute and solo set on either channel apply to
both, and the editor mirrors the pan (the vendor's panel's behaviour there
was never captured).
.TP
.BR "monitor gain" " \fIdB\fR [" left | right ]
The MONITOR OUT level: both sides, or the side given (while linked, both
sides follow anyway).
.TP
.BR "monitor link" " " on | off
.TP
.BR "panel mono" | soft\-ctrl " " on | off
.TP
.BR "panel output\-volume" " " main | all
Whether the OUTPUT VOLUME knob acts on the main outputs or on all of them.
.TP
.BR "panel clock" " " internal | ext\-spdif | ext\-adat
Selecting the ADAT clock also selects the ADAT digital output.
.TP
.BR "panel digital\-out" " " spdif | adat
.TP
.BR route " " wave\-in | output\-dm\-on | output\-dm\-off " \fIpair source\fR"
Route a source to what the computer records
.RB ( wave\-in )
or to an output, with direct monitor on or all off.
Sources:
.BR monitor\-out ,
.BR input\-1\-2 " .. " input\-9\-10 ,
.BR wave\-out\-1\-2 " .. " wave\-out\-9\-10 ,
and for
.B output\-dm\-off
also
.BR same .
.TP
.BR "preset wave\-in" " " initial | loop\-back
.TP
.BR "preset output" " " initial | hard\-thru | monitor\-out
.TP
.BR memory " " store | load " \fIslot\fR [\fIsections\fR]"
Slots 1 to 5.
.I sections
is
.B all
(the default) or a comma-separated list of
.BR panel ,
.BR wave\-in ,
.BR output
and
.BR monitor .
Loading a slot the editor did not store leaves the editor's mixer settings
unknown, and says so; the state file records it
.RB ( "mixer unknown" ,
shown by
.BR status )
until a
.B sync
or a load of a slot the editor stored.
After a load that includes the panel or the patch bay, the device reports
them, and the state follows.
.TP
.BI user\-set " rate"
Store the power-on settings: the panel switches, at
.I rate
Hz (44100, 48000, 88200 or 96000).
Read back from the device, the USER SET also holds the patch bay and both
mixers as they were when it was stored, and with the SAMPLE RATE switch on
USER SET the device powers up in exactly that state.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the device or the state file fails (also when another
program, such as a connected
.BR ua1000gui ,
holds the MIDI port), 2 for a command line that is not understood.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ua1000/state
The editor's state, or
.I ~/.local/state/ua1000/state
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is not set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
ua1000ctl input 1 gain \-6
ua1000ctl output 3\-4 link on
ua1000ctl route wave\-in 1\-2 monitor\-out
ua1000ctl \-n panel mono on
.EE
.SH NOTES
The device listens on USB-MIDI cable 1; the kernel's
.B snd\-ua101
driver must address that cable for any of this to reach it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ua1000gui (1),
.BR amidi (1)
